{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.fixed_word_concentration","record":{"id":"circuit.fixed_word_concentration","kind":"claim","label":"Fixed-word SK QAOA first and second measurement moments","status":"unverified","source_id":"fggz2022_fixed_depth","statement":"For every fixed finite depth p and fixed real angle vectors gamma and beta, with standard Gaussian SK couplings and the usual plus-state QAOA circuit, let Y_n=H_n(sigma)/n for a computational-basis outcome sigma. Over the joint law of disorder and measurement, E[Y_n] tends to v_p(gamma,beta) and E[Y_n^2] tends to v_p(gamma,beta)^2 as n tends to infinity.","assumption_ids":["assumptions.qaoa_model_and_limits"],"evidence_ids":["concentration_source_warrant_pending"],"citation_ids":["fggz2022_fixed_depth"],"boundary":"External ordinary theorem input, source-interface checked only; not a new Lean declaration or independently replayed proof. The word is fixed before n tends to infinity. The second moment is the quantum second moment averaged over disorder, not only the squared conditional energy expectation. No uniform rate over depth or angles is asserted.","locator":"Farhi-Goldstone-Gutmann-Zhou, arXiv:1910.08187v4, section 4 theorem, equations (13)-(14)."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.fixed_word_concentration","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/qaoa-quantifiers","anchor":"qaoa-quantifiers","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/qaoa-quantifiers","anchor":"qaoa-quantifiers","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n\n<a id=\"fixed-word-probability\"></a>\n\n### 11.2 What is averaged, and a fixed-word probability consequence\n\nThe probability conclusion here uses an additional fixed-word second-moment\ntheorem, beyond the expected-value construction of §§8.1–8.6.\nThe theorem in §8 is an existence statement about limiting expected\nenergy. There are two random choices behind that expectation. First the\ncouplings $J$ are sampled; then a computational-basis measurement of the\nstate produces a spin string $\\Sigma$. For the single word $W$ already\nchosen, put\n$$\nE_n(J,\\Sigma)=H_{n,J}(\\Sigma)/n,\\qquad\n\\overline E_n(J)=\\mathbb E[E_n(J,\\Sigma)\\mid J].\n$$\nThe quantity defining $v_W$ is $\\mathbb E_J\\overline E_n(J)$.\nIt is neither the energy of every measured string nor a worst-disorder\nguarantee. The temporary Gaussian labels used during synthesis are not\nan extra source of randomness in the final angle vectors.\n\nFarhi, Goldstone, Gutmann and Zhou supply that additional input. They prove, with\nthe same Hamiltonian and gate-sign convention, that for every fixed word\n$$\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\Sigma}E_n\\longrightarrow v_W,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\Sigma}E_n^2\\longrightarrow v_W^2.\n$$\nTheir second expression is the quantum expectation of $(C_{n,J}/n)^2$\naveraged over disorder, not merely the square of the conditional mean.\nConsequently\n$$\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\Sigma}(E_n-v_W)^2\\longrightarrow0,\n\\qquad\n\\Pr_{J,\\Sigma}(|E_n-v_W|>s)\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad(s>0).\n$$\nThe law of total variance separates what vanishes:\n$$\n\\mathbb E(E_n-v_W)^2=\n\\mathbb E_J\\operatorname{Var}(E_n\\mid J)\n +\\mathbb E_J(\\overline E_n(J)-v_W)^2.\n$$\nBoth terms are nonnegative and hence tend to zero. Concentration of the\nconditional mean alone would not control a measured string.\nThis is the elementary second-moment deduction from [Farhi et al.,\narXiv:1910.08187v4, §4, equations (13)–(22)][fggz].\n\nNow run the circuit construction with accuracy $\\varepsilon/2$ and\nfreeze its resulting word. Since $v_W\\ge P_*-\\varepsilon/2$,\n$$\n\\Pr_{J,\\Sigma}\\{E_n<P_*-\\varepsilon\\}\n\\le \\frac{4}{\\varepsilon^2}\n       \\mathbb E_{J,\\Sigma}(E_n-v_W)^2\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nThis is a consequence of the expected-value construction **together\nwith** the external second-moment theorem. It is not a clause of either\nselected formal interface. If\n$b_n(J)=\\Pr(E_n<P_*-\\varepsilon\\mid J)$, then for every fixed\n$\\tau>0$,\n$$\n\\Pr_J\\{b_n(J)>\\tau\\}\n\\le\\mathbb E_J b_n(J)/\\tau\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nThus the same word also has small conditional measurement-failure\nprobability for most disorder instances, in this precise sense. No\nclaim is made for every instance, for arbitrarily varying depths, or\nwith a quantitative size threshold.\n\nFor $K$ fresh state preparations at the same disorder instance, the\nconditional failures are independent, so their joint failure probability\nis $\\mathbb E_J b_n(J)^K$, not $(\\mathbb E_J b_n(J))^K$.\nA fraction $\\alpha$ of instances that always fail gives $\\alpha$ for\nthe former at every $K$. A bound $b_n(J)\\le\\tau$ on a particular\ninstance does give $\\tau^K$ there. Repeatedly measuring an already\ncollapsed state is a different experiment.\n\n","span":{"start_byte":221348,"end_byte":224449,"start_line":4784,"end_line":4861},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=qaoa-quantifiers","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=qaoa-quantifiers"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=circuit.fixed_word_concentration&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=circuit.fixed_word_concentration&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=circuit.fixed_word_concentration&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=circuit.fixed_word_concentration&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"field:20300ce3b687b98ced4639ea04f4f98996c1d4683a4d82fdf899d2a2ed3d63d4","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.fixed_word_concentration","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/assumptions.qaoa_model_and_limits","kind":"assumption_ids","semantic_class":"explicit_assumption_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.fixed_word_concentration","source_field":"assumption_ids"},{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/edges/%5B%22circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum%22%2C%22circuit